**programme.lv is**
- An online judge - system to host programming tasks
- A tool for educators & guide for aspirants

Objectives:
- Lower barriers: address beginner obstacles (e.g., IDE setup)
- Archive tasks: persist and publish high-quality contest tasks
- Support teachers: tools to create/share tasks, monitor progress
- Foster community: host contests, plan in-person events

Strategies:
- Make it fun: engaging sounds/visuals, fun task formulations, easter eggs
- Guide beginners: step-by-step IDE setup guide, link to other resources online
- Ranking system: XP awards and competitive activity rankings
- Community: organize meetups, lectures, enable discussions

Value proposition:
- Accessible, easier tasks for general education
- Private tasks for teachers to share with student groups
- Integrated web-based code editor
- Partial solution support with subtasks
- Support for most popular programming languages

Revenue Streams:
- Premium content: advanced courses via partners
- Sponsorships: badges, tasks featuring logos
- Donations and grants for STEM education

Metrics for Success:
- Active users (daily/monthly uniques)
- Archive coverage (Latvian Olympiads tasks)
- Retention (30-day return rate)

Background:
- J. Gruzis develops CleverCode as a bachelor thesis (2014), maintains it until 2021
- In 2018, author discovers CleverCode, self-learns programming
- In 2022, struggles to find tasks to assign in as a teacher to pupils

Technical challenges:
- Safe solution testing system
- Broader programming language support
- Systematized task archive
- Subtasks and test groups
- Real-time submission result streaming
- Support for interactive tasks